Yoga Vāsiṣṭha – Brahma Gītā
Sarga 186 (Part 13)
The Seer, the Seen, and Seeing Are One
"The seer and the seen are one, for Consciousness is all-pervading."
Sri Vasiṣṭha now takes us to the very summit of Advaita.
If Consciousness alone pervades everything, who is the observer? What is the object? What is the act of perception?
The answer is breathtakingly simple.
They are all expressions of the same limitless Awareness.
Just as movement cannot be separated from air,
and fluidity cannot be separated from water,
the universe cannot be separated from Brahman.
The world is not a second reality standing beside the Absolute.
It is Brahman appearing as the universe.
Then comes one of the great declarations of the Yoga Vāsiṣṭha:
"I alone am that Cosmic Self; this entire universe is the body of the Virāt."
There is no more separation between Brahman and the world than there is between space and openness.
Sri Vasiṣṭha illustrates this beautifully.
Countless droplets leap from a waterfall, each seeming separate for a moment, yet all remain nothing but water.
Likewise, innumerable beings, worlds, moments, and histories appear and disappear within Consciousness without ever departing from It.
The Witness never becomes divided.
It simply shines as the knower,
the known,
and the knowing.
This is the vision of non-duality.
Realization does not erase the world.
It dissolves the illusion that anything has ever existed apart from Brahman.
Hari Om Tat Sat
